# Why ...

## ... use `pnpm`?

`pnpm` uses a cache and is more efficient than `npm`.

## ... use `github`?

It is pretty ubiquitous and virtually a standard for managing Foundry modules.

## ... use `vite`?

It provides nice tools (like an \{{HMR}} development server) out of the box.

## ... use `vitest`?

Clean, simple, flexible, and ties in to the `vite` ecosystem.

## ... use `docker`?

For ancillary tooling it provides a neat way to isolate from the host (so we can provide neat features without needing
to have you install a load of stuff). It also provides a nicely isolated environment for running development Foundry
VTT servers, which can also be used in more advanced scenarios in `.github/workflows`.

## ... document with Sphinx?

This is just my taste. I like the power and simplicity of reStructuredText,
but have provided Markdown as a more widely adopted standard (both have a
shallower learning curve than, for example, LaTeX). I also like the flexible
output method from Sphinx. (See [](#replacing-sphinx))
{{#if svelte}}

## ... all the `svelte` stuff?

This module template assumes you will be using [svelte](https://svelte.dev) for any UI (rather than the Foundry
Handlebars). {program}`Svelte` is (\{{IMHO}}) a more powerful, more reactive, yet simpler, UI framework. It plays nicely
with `vite` and together they produce small modules. One can use a complete library, such as
[TyphonJS](https://github.com/typhonjs-fvtt-lib) (which attempts to replicate the Foundry API UI as Svelte components),
or simply wrap standard Svelte by extending
[ApplicationV2](https://foundryvtt.com/api/v14/classes/foundry.applications.api.ApplicationV2.html). Which is better
depends on your objectives.
{{/if}}
{{#if lit}}

## ... all the `lit` stuff?

This module template assumes you will be using [Lit](https://lit.dev) for any UI (rather than the Foundry Handlebars).
{program}`Lit` is a small library for writing web components, which are part of the browser itself. A component is a
custom element, so it works wherever HTML does, including inside a window built by extending
[ApplicationV2](https://foundryvtt.com/api/v14/classes/foundry.applications.api.ApplicationV2.html), and it needs no
compiler of its own, so `vite` bundles it without a plugin.

Custom element names are shared by everything on the page. Start yours with the module ID, as in
`{{{moduleId}}}-panel`, to keep clear of other modules.

{{#if typescript}}
`tsconfig.json` enables decorators (`@customElement`, `@property`) the way {program}`Lit` recommends: with
`experimentalDecorators`, and with `useDefineForClassFields` off so that class fields do not hide reactive properties.
{{else}}
Declare reactive properties in a `static properties` field; decorators are not yet part of the language.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
